Best director.

3
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Best director.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,000
And what else?

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Best sound.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Best sound?

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Yeah.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
And also...

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:38,000
You mean like editing or something like that?

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,000
I think it was, yeah.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Might have been editing, yeah.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Something like that.

13
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Yeah, but it won like four or five awards.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:43,680
But for me, it was the best picture of the year.

15
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,280
Oh, best original screenplay, right?

16
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:46,280
Best original screenplay.

17
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:47,280
Oh, yeah.

18
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:48,280
True.

19
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:49,280
That's true.

20
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:50,280
It was a really good movie.

21
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:53,480
I only saw it recently and I was pleasantly surprised how good the movie was.

22
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:58,560
Yeah, and I've heard from a lot of people who were ex-military or who have served abroad

23
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:03,800
that this really captures part of their experience and you never hear that about war movies.

24
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:09,120
Usually war movies are very romanticized, you know, the hero and this one, there was

25
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:10,360
no romance.

26
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:13,280
It was all hard.

27
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,560
Very challenging movie.

28
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,120
Without too much politics, too.

29
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:19,260
Actually, I think that was the key of the movie.

30
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:24,000
They never, at any point, criticize or, you know...

31
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Are in favor of...

32
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,480
Exactly in favor or against, nothing.

33
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,920
Did you see people doing their job, what they're supposed to do?

34
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:37,560
And I think what the movie was, the best part of it is that you see how people get addicted

35
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:40,080
to war, to the adrenaline rush.

36
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:42,960
They're just like adrenaline junkies, you know?

37
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,600
So basically, the movie is set in Iraq.

38
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:47,600
That's right.

39
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:53,000
And we are following a group of soldiers that belong to the bomb squad.

40
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,120
So basically, they go around, they receive calls or maybe information about a bomb, maybe

41
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:02,400
in a car or something like that, and they have to go check it out, and if it is a bomb,

42
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:07,400
they have to take it apart, disarm it, and, you know, make sure that no one is hurt.

43
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:15,360
So we're following around this group of guys who obviously have a very dangerous job, right?

44
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:20,720
Because at any time, this bomb can explode because also sometimes they wait until the

45
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:22,960
people arrive to make it explode, right?

46
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:23,960
That's right.

47
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:24,960
And these are not just any bombs.

48
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:29,160
These are IEDs, improvised explosive devices.

49
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,680
That basically means that they're homemade.

50
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:37,240
And there's an extra element of danger with IEDs, as we see in the news a lot, because

51
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:43,680
they're very unstable, and the rules are different for disarming an IED than for

52
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:44,680
a traditional bomb.

53
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:45,680
Right.

54
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:48,280
So just imagine it's like a homemade television, right?

55
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:53,440
You don't have any previous information about the circuits, how it's built, or how

56
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:55,680
it's supposed to detonate.

57
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:01,260
It's basically, there's a high probability that it will explode because you have no real

58
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:03,600
information about what the bomb will be like.

59
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:08,720
Yeah, that's why they always, well, at the beginning, the first guy, which is Guy Pierce,

60
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:15,480
which I was surprised to see him in such, you know, like a short period of the movie.

61
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,640
He goes in, instead of going himself, he sends, you know, the little card to see first

62
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:22,240
if it's going to explode.

63
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:26,560
But then the main guy comes, and he just goes for it, you know?

64
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:28,240
He doesn't wait for it.

65
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:29,240
It's just insane.

66
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:30,240
It's scary.

67
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:33,480
Yeah, he's kind of very adventurous.

68
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:34,480
I don't know.

69
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:36,680
I think that's part of the friction with the movie.

70
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:41,120
His style is very different, so he's not as precautious.

71
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:45,880
So his teammates are a little bit anxious whenever he's doing these things, like handling

72
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:48,800
explosives in such an adventurous way.

73
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,040
He seems careless.

74
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:51,040
He seems careless.

75
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:55,200
And for the people around him, it makes them nervous because carelessness can mean death

76
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,320
because they're at war.

77
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:02,200
But this character, that's part of the problem, I think, with the challenge of the movie.

78
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:06,680
It's a very psychological movie because you have to ask, why is this man so careless?

79
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:08,120
He has a family.

80
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:09,540
He has friends.

81
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:14,260
That makes him act this way because normal people care about their lives, right?

82
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:15,640
You don't want to die.

83
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,740
But it seems like he doesn't really care.

84
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:19,220
But then that's the interesting part.

85
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:21,180
The whole movie unfolds.

86
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:23,220
It's not that much of a...

87
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:25,780
It's not very fictitious, so to say.

88
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:27,380
It's very real life.

89
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:32,100
The brotherhood between soldiers, the conflicts that they could have, how they care for

90
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:34,460
one another, how they have friction.

91
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:37,540
Because obviously, when you spend that much time with people, sometimes you have problems,

92
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:38,540
right?

93
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:40,500
Like arguments or maybe whatever.

94
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:41,500
Sorry.

95
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:47,780
So, yeah, it's a very good psychological aspect of the movie.

96
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:55,020
I think the best part of it is you really grasp this notion of how much they're counting

97
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:57,220
every day.

98
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:01,460
It's good because in the movie, they always put at the beginning, how many days they

99
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:03,940
have to be stationed in that part.

100
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:08,380
So they're always counting, okay, 34 days left.

101
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:11,940
But then you see this, you know, how it's constant.

102
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:15,740
Every time it's like, okay, yeah, it's just 34 days.

103
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:17,580
But maybe they go back, you know?

104
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:19,940
Yeah, but it doesn't seem to get any easier.

105
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:20,940
Exactly.

106
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:24,960
So it's like 34 days, you would think it's harder than five days until they leave.

107
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:29,260
But you see that every day, it's the same risk and the same dangers.

108
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:32,780
It's like even the last day of work, something could go wrong.

109
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:34,700
Well, and that's the motif of the movie.

110
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:37,280
At a very basic level, it's about time.

111
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:41,320
As you think about a bomb, you have a certain amount of time to disarm the bomb.

112
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:46,720
The clock is always going down, five, four, three, two, and these men's lives and women,

113
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:52,160
their lives is all about time, about time in Iraq, time home, time disarming a bomb.

114
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:54,840
And so the movie is very anxious.

115
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:59,160
It gives you a feeling of anxiety, because there's always the time elements.

116
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:00,680
There's never enough time.

117
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:06,640
And it's very effective in that way, because more than any other war movie, it makes the

118
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:10,720
environment sound horrible.

119
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:16,380
If you see it, you really feel like you yourself, the viewer, is in danger.

120
