175
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:53,020
So interesting enough, this guy may seem careless, but he disarmed 873 bombs.

176
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:55,020
He's very good at what he does.

177
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:56,020
Yeah.

178
00:07:56,020 --> 00:08:01,020
But imagine feeling 873 times in your life that you're going to die.

179
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:05,020
I mean, either he feels it or he doesn't, you know, at some point.

180
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:07,020
Maybe he feels it, but he likes it.

181
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:11,020
I think that's exactly the point, that he likes it.

182
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:14,020
It's like a drug, you know, it's like cocaine or something.

183
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:15,020
He gets high on that.

184
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:16,020
Yeah, really.

185
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:21,020
Because you know how there's people that they need to do something so intense

186
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:23,020
in order to feel alive?

187
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:24,020
Right.

188
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:25,020
So maybe that's it.

189
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:27,020
But you can't live with that.

190
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:29,020
You know, when the war is over, what do you do?

191
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:30,020
You become a mercenary.

192
00:08:30,020 --> 00:08:34,020
Do you, I mean, you can't have that on all the time.

193
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:35,020
You can't have relationships.

194
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:37,020
You can't live like a normal human.

195
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:41,020
So the question is, and this comes up towards the end of the film,

196
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:43,020
what do you do afterwards?

197
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:45,020
How do you live your life?

198
00:08:45,020 --> 00:08:48,020
And I think that this is something the writers did really well.

199
00:08:48,020 --> 00:08:50,020
And I think it's worth mentioning.

200
00:08:50,020 --> 00:08:54,020
The writer of this movie was what we call an embedded journalist.

201
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:57,020
He was living with the military in Iraq.

202
00:08:57,020 --> 00:08:59,020
And so he lived with these people.

203
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:03,020
He was not in the military, but he lived with these people

204
00:09:03,020 --> 00:09:06,020
and he was with them every day and he saw how things went.

205
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:08,020
And then he went back as well to the United States.

206
00:09:08,020 --> 00:09:12,020
And I think that that really informed his perspective on this kind of life.

207
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:14,020
I think you get used to it, right?

208
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:16,020
You get used to a certain lifestyle, certain surroundings.

209
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:20,020
And then once you're out of that, everything else seems unfamiliar.

210
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:22,020
And I think it's really interesting because,

211
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:25,020
actually I was talking about this the other day.

212
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:28,020
One of the famous race car drivers, Michael Schumacher,

213
00:09:28,020 --> 00:09:31,020
recently came out of retirement to go back to driving, right?

214
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:33,020
And everyone's like, why?

215
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:35,020
He's like 41 years old.

216
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:37,020
He's like a seven-time world champion.

217
00:09:37,020 --> 00:09:39,020
Why would he do that and risk his life again

218
00:09:39,020 --> 00:09:41,020
going in a car at 300 km per hour?

219
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:43,020
But this is who he is.

220
00:09:43,020 --> 00:09:46,020
I think you need it, right?

221
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:48,020
There's no other explanation.

222
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:50,020
It's not like he needs the money.

223
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:52,020
It's not like he needs the fame.

224
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:55,020
I think it's just one of those things that is with you

225
00:09:55,020 --> 00:09:59,020
and he will probably be happy to die within a race car

226
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:00,020
because that's what he loves doing.

227
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:01,020
Yeah, it's true.

228
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:03,020
I think that's the way people are.

229
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:07,020
Well, as one more side note to this conversation and to the movie,

230
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:09,020
I think it's worth mentioning that the director

231
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:12,020
is the first woman to win an Academy Award.

232
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:16,020
Catherine Bigelow, she did an amazing job in this movie.

233
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:20,020
And I think everyone should go to Wikipedia or to IMDB

234
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:23,020
and to read more about her because she's really inspirational.

235
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:27,020
So she's the first woman in history

236
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:29,020
to win an Academy Award for Best Director.

237
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:30,020
That's right.

238
00:10:30,020 --> 00:10:31,020
Nice.

239
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:33,020
And she won it on a war movie.

240
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:35,020
I mean, breaking down these stereotypes, right?

241
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:36,020
I know.

242
00:10:36,020 --> 00:10:40,020
It's not like the lovey-dovey kissy drama type like that.

243
00:10:40,020 --> 00:10:41,020
Historical drama.

244
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:45,020
Has there been any other war movie that's won an award like this

245
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:46,020
for Best Picture?

246
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:47,020
Lots for Best Picture.

247
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:48,020
For Best Picture?

248
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:49,020
Oh, Platoon.

249
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:50,020
Yeah, you're right.

